  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0b/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_12.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man drops by the <i>Daily Bugle</i> office to complain about his coverage and notes that Betty has returned as Jameson's secretary.&nbsp; The <i>Bugle</i> accuses Spider-Man of allowing Dr. Octopus to escape in Philadelphia (last issue).&nbsp; Now Ock is on a crime spree \"thruout [sic] the nation.\"&nbsp; Ock is <i>trying</i> to goad Spider-Man into attacking him, <i>certain</i> that he's more powerful.&nbsp; But when Spider-Man doesn't immediately come after him, Ock decides to return to NYC and find <i>him</i> instead!\n\nOck has no way of knowing that the <i>reason</i> Spider-Man hasn't hunted him down is because, at that moment, Peter Parker is at home and seems close to catching a cold.&nbsp; (Plus, he can't afford the fare to get out to where Dr. Octopus is, not to mention final exams are coming up at his school very soon.)\n\nAt the Bugle, Betty answers the phone, not realizing that it's Ock, who is only calling to ensure she's there.&nbsp; He'll use <i>her</i> as bait for Spider-Man.&nbsp; And indeed he <i>does</i> arrive for her, just after Parker comes to the office to walk Betty home.&nbsp; Ock demands that Jameson print a note in the paper, telling Spider-Man to meet him at Coney Island!&nbsp; If he wants the girl, he must come <i>alone</i>, but he'll allow one photographer to take pictures of the defeat.&nbsp; Jameson turns to Parker and tags him for the job!\n\nPeter is getting increasingly woozy from the oncoming illness, which is affecting his ability to cling to walls, but nothing will stop him from getting to Coney Island!&nbsp; But he's obviously feeling very weak.&nbsp; When he lunges at Ock to try to throw a punch, the blow is barely felt by his opponent, who thinks it's a trick!&nbsp; Ock is easily able to handle the weakened Spider-Man and even says \"You're like a human punching bag!!&nbsp; What's <i>happened</i> to you?\" and later, \"He doesn't even struggle as I try to remove his mask!&nbsp; There can only be <i>one </i>answer...\"\n\nAnd he rips off Spider-Man's mask!&nbsp; \"I should have <i>known</i>!!&nbsp; It <i>isn't</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; It's that weakling brat, Peter Parker!\"&nbsp; The onlookers are amazed!&nbsp; Peter could have been killed!&nbsp; The police get him home, where hours later, Aunt May has summoned the doctor, who assures her that it's only the \"twenty-four hour virus,\" and that he'll be fine by morning.\n\nAnd sure enough, when he wakes up, he's good as new.&nbsp; At school, his classmates have all heard that he impersonated Spider-Man to try and rescue Betty, for which Liz thinks he's wonderful, but Flash is sure Peter only did it to show off, because he was sure he wouldn't actually run into Ock.&nbsp; Liz tells him off!\n\nMeanwhile, Ock is furious that the papers are saying he was fooled by a teenager and allowed his hostage to go free, all without ever having to <i>fight</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; He releases the large animals from the zoo to terrorize the city.&nbsp; That occupies the police and Spider-Man while he continues his rampage.\n\nFinally, Ock dislodges a sign on the side of a building, hoping that threatening the citizens below will finally draw out the web-slinger.&nbsp; It does, and with that, the battle begins!\n\nPages of battle action ensue, with Spider-Man pointing out \"You're not fighting a weak <i>imitation</i> of Spider-Man now... this time you're facing the <i>real thing</i>!\"&nbsp; Eventually the two crash into a \"deserted sculptor's studio\" where some spilled cleaning fluid starts a fire.\n\nSpider-Man points out that they both need to get out of there, but Ock is so livid at his enemy that he pays no heed, which leads to him getting stuck under a fallen sculpture.&nbsp; Before Spider-Man can pull him out, the floor collapses under the weight, and Ock disappears from view.\n\nBut Spider-Man is in no position to worry about it, as the flames inch ever closer to <i>him</i>!&nbsp; He eventually fashions a web shield and leaps through an open window to safety.&nbsp; Soon, the fire department arrives and drags a weakened Doc Ock out into the hands of the waiting police.\n\nOn the street below, Peter Parker now watches and turns down a request from Liz Allen to attend a party, but he's got a date with Betty -- she can have <i>Flash</i> instead!&nbsp; He even remarks that they <i>deserve</i> each other.&nbsp; Liz realizes that she probably deserves that treatment after the way they've treated him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Spider-Man's shocking unmasking delivers exactly the moment the cover promises.\nUnfortunately, the rest of the issue struggles to be nearly as memorable.",
  "subtitle": "Unmasked?&nbsp; Or Already Finished?",
  "review_text": "It seemed apparent right from the very start of this offering that it wasn't going to be as involving as previous issues had been.&nbsp; There was something <i>way</i> too mundane about its atmosphere.\n\n[ASSET: left | CatchingACold | width=21%] Indeed, the opening pages lacked the flair we've seen, and about the only thing new we learn is Betty is back at the <i>Daily Bugle</i>, that Dr. Octopus is trying to draw Spider-Man to him (for purposes of revenge), and that Peter is catching a cold.&nbsp; (And this, after last issue's bum ankle -- the guy can't catch a <i>break</i>!)\n[BR]\n[BR] You'll note also in this panel how he contemplates the fact that he has no money to go after Doc Ock, even if he <i>was</i> healthy.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=(I guess he must have used all his available cash when he took the plane to Philadelphia last month!)]\n\nRecall that near the end of the previous issue, Doc Ock escaped after the speed boat he and Spider-Man were battling on crashed into a piling near Philadelphia.\n\n[ASSET: right | HowDoesHeKnow | width=14%] When Ock sensed that Spider-Man <i>wasn't</i> going to seek him out, he decided to kidnap Betty in order to entice Spider-Man to chase after him (he did, after all, try the same thing on page 18 of last month's issue).&nbsp; It was a sensible enough plan, and he <i>had</i> met Betty Brant when she picked him up from prison (last issue, page 4).&nbsp; That part, at least, made sense.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=But how on Earth did Ock know that Betty had worked at the <i>Daily Bugle</i>?]\n[BR] Or that she had now <i>returned</i> to that job?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(We're forced to assume that they must have chatted during their car ride from NYC to Philly.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | TooEasy | width=10%] But it was time to get to the main event, so without a lot of hoopla (or intrigue), the woozy Spider-Man attacks Dr. Octopus, but has little left in his tank.\n[BR]\n[BR] His efforts prove <i>so feeble</i> that even Octopus refuses to believe this is the genuine article!\n\nBefore you knew it, Spider-Man was overwhelmed... and unmasked... by a surprised Doc Ock!\n[ASSET: center | Unmasked | width=large]\n\nAnd thus, the cover image proved to be true enough:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"Not a Dream!&nbsp; Not an Imaginary Tale!\"]\n\n[ASSET: left | ZillionBucksAgain | width=15%] [ASSET: right | LizAllanWakesUp | width=15%] It was a clever enough twist, but with a mere wave of their hands, the co-creators brushed the whole thing off by describing Peter's virus as the 24-hour variety, and by the next morning, the young hero is back on his feet.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Though I did at least like the way his futile attempt at bravery awoke Liz Allan to the fact that Peter was courageous.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nSadly, most of the rest of the issue was <i>far</i> more mundane, and I particularly <b>disliked</b> the decision by Doc Ock to release the large zoo animals.&nbsp; He seemed so intent on being taken seriously as a genuine threat to the city that releasing the animals felt almost random, even if he later tries to justify it as a way of drawing Spider-Man out.\n\nWhat it <i>really </i>did, though, was essentially waste about three pages of story, which featured little more than the title hero battling lions and gorillas.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Excuse me, but that's <i>hardly</i> compelling stuff!]\n\nBut what <i>did</i> work was the wonderful page 14 sequence where Doc Ock dislodged a sign on the side of the building that read \"<b>LEEDIT Inc</b>\" -- a <i>painfully</i> obvious amalgamation of the names \"Lee\" and \"Ditko\" - [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=too funny!]\n[ASSET: center | LeeDitSign | width=large]\nIn retrospect, I suppose it <i>could</i> have also been \"LEEDIT <b>Co</b>\" (short for \"Company\" instead of \"Incorporated\"), but perhaps that might have been a [SPAN: severity=info; text=little <i>too</i> obvious!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Still, it would have been even <i>funnier</i> in my opinion...)]\n\n[ASSET: left | NoImitationThisTime | width=13%] It was also good to see Spider-Man \"sell\" the illusion that Ock hadn't actually faced him earlier when he exclaims (during the battle),\n[BR]\n[BR] \"You're not fighting a weak <i>imitation</i> of Spider-Man now...\n[BR] ...this time you're facing the <i>real thing</i>!\" [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=(Nicely done!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | HauledAway | width=13%] What followed was about 6 to 7 pages of largely lackluster action, resulting in the \"half-dead\" Dr. Octopus being dragged out of the burning building, handcuffed, and ushered back to prison.\n[BR]\n[BR] All I can say is that, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he sure must have been half dead not to have resisted arrest!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You'd think that anyone who can go toe to toe with Spider-Man could handle a couple of policemen!)]\n\nThe issue closes with Liz now thinking Peter is <i>great</i>, but he's too busy planning his next date with Betty Brant to give her the time of day.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThere is only one word I can think of to describe this issue:&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"Average\"]\n\nIt's a story that only occasionally rises above its one gimmick, that of showing the ever-Amazing Spider-Man being unmasked in public!\n\n\nSay what you want about the unmasking:\n[HANG] * Peter was sick, so no one believed he was really Spider-Man\n[HANG] * the fact that it <i>sort of</i> felt like a cheat in the end\n\nBut you can't argue that a cover like that <i>had</i> to have elicited sales!\n[BR] Especially one that went out of its way to scream:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Hey everyone!&nbsp; This is no <i>trick!</i>  This is <i>for real</i>!\"]&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Note that the cover of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #121 has a similar blurb, which is probably why I immediately took note of it on the cover of <i>this</i> issue!&nbsp; I suppose X-Men #42 does as well.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=In other words, the gimmick <b><i>worked!</i></b>]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And you have to at <i>least</i> tip your cap to Marvel for that!)]\n\nOne can't help wondering if this wasn't in direct response to what was commonplace in many DC Comics of that era.\n\nOf course, the circumstances were carefully orchestrated by the co-creators so that the villain thinks <b><i>he's</i></b><i> </i>been tricked.&nbsp; (But at least the cover wasn't lying, and that's <i>something</i>.)\n\nYet, once the shocking unmasking had happened (on page 8), [SPAN: severity=info; text=there was still two-thirds of a book left to fill,] and from that point on, the story lacked the same \"zingarooo\" as what came before.&nbsp; (That was the word Peter used after he woke up the next morning feeling loads better.)\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=The co-creators seemingly forgot to give Doc Ock much in the way of motivation.]&nbsp; Last month saw him going after nothing but cold, hard cash, only engaging with Spider-Man when he <i>interfered</i>, but this month, he seemed more interested in <i>retribution</i>.\n\nThat is so very \"Dr. Doom\" of him, or for that matter, any Marvel villain who's overly fixated on \"getting revenge\" on their arch nemesis.\n\nI <i>understand</i> this is the unstated motif ([SPAN: fontsize=small; text=dare I say \"trope\"?]) in the genre of comics, but it doesn't mean that Marvel must blindly follow the formula.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=I expect <i>more</i>!]\n\nAs for the supporting characters, other than Liz Allan (whose character was obviously in focus this month), they did very little.&nbsp; Aunt May acted as she always does.&nbsp; Flash Thompson did the same.&nbsp; So did Jameson.&nbsp; They all seemed \"present,\" even if they didn't add much to the story, making their appearances more <i>perfunctory</i> than actually <i>necessary</i>.\n\nI noted in my review of <i>last</i> month's issue that they didn't <i>all</i> need to show up, so long as the ones who <i>do</i> serve some <i>purpose</i> in the story.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=Unfortunately, that seems to have changed this time.]\n\n[HANG0] Is this new Liz Allan's outlook to be only a one-issue attitude adjustment?\n[BR] Or is she <i>really</i> seeing Peter in another light?\n[BR] (That is admittedly some amount of intrigue worth coming back next month for!)\n\nBut supporting cast aside, [SPAN: severity=info; text=even Doc Ock seemed only present to satisfy the promise of the cover.]&nbsp; But when it came right down to the battle, one wonders if he was even necessary to the plot either, or if he could have been replaced by <i>any</i> of Spider-Man's other villains.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=\"I'll get you, Spider-Man!\"] is simply not enough to drive a plot.\n\nSpider-Man heals, catches the villain, who is captured, and life goes on.\n\nLike I said, it never rose above <b>average</b>, one with no notable <i>faults</i>, mind you, but also not life-altering in any way.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=That's a cocktail that likely results in a story only remembered in the long run for that one, cover moment.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Page 10 shows Peter wondering about \"Liz Allen,\" which is misspelled in this issue, as her name is actually \"Liz Allan.\"\n\n[HANG0] On page 20, when inside the sculptor's studio, Ock calls out that, \"This building is deserted!\"\n[BR] Boy, all these \"deserted\" or \"abandoned\" or \"condemned\" buildings must just <i>litter</i> Manhattan -- <i>Wow!</i>\n\n[HANG0] The <b>Special Announcements Section</b> of this issue [LINK:1964:ASM_12|ASM #12|2027-06-02] explains that Betty is actually <i>younger</i> than Peter, having had to leave school and get a job because she needed the money, intending to complete her education when she gets a chance.&nbsp; By the way, that announcement goes on to declare Peter as a \"high school senior.\")"
  ],
  "letters_page": "The \"Spider's Web\" page contains a letter from future comic book creator Dave Cockrum.",
  "highlight": "Doc Ock gets the unmasking everyone paid money to see.&nbsp; Then... there's still most of a comic left to read."
}